  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’s article in The Journal of Hellenic 
Studies presents the primary publication of the gold signet ring known as the 
“Ring of Nestor” and related finds from a sepulchral deposit at Thisbê in 
Boeotia. Evans describes the discovery context of a treasure of gold signet 
rings and bead seals from a tomb, highlighting the ring’s association within 
this funerary assemblage. He provides a detailed physical description of the 
Ring of Nestor, noting its gold material, signet form, and complex multi‑figured
iconographic scene. Drawing on comparisons with Minoan glyptic from Knossos and 
other Aegean sites, Evans argues for a Minoan origin and Late Bronze Age date 
for the ring. He interprets the scene as a ritual or funerary depiction 
connected to notions of the Minoan “after‑world,” including possible chthonic or
underworld elements, and uses the ring to support broader ideas about Minoan 
religious beliefs and practices. The article has subsequently become a 
foundational reference for later scholarship on the ring’s discovery, 
description, dating, cultural attribution, and iconography.",
      "source_ref": "s1"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d2",
      "document_summary": "The edited conference volume Current Approaches and 
New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography includes repeated citation of Evans’s 
1925 article “The Ring of Nestor” as a classic study of Minoan and Mycenaean 
gold ring iconography. Within the volume, Evans’s publication is used as a key 
reference point for discussions of ritual and sacred imagery on gold rings, and 
contributors engage with his dating and interpretation of the Ring of Nestor. 
Some chapters adopt, refine, or challenge aspects of Evans’s views on Minoan 
religion and glyptic imagery, situating the Ring of Nestor within broader 
debates over the reading of ritual scenes and sacred spaces on Aegean rings. The
volume thus provides modern historiographic framing of the Ring of Nestor and 
demonstrates its continued importance in contemporary iconographic and 
methodological debates.",
      "source_ref": "s2"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d3",
      "document_summary": "The paper titled “Some observations on the 
iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor’” offers a focused scholarly analysis of the 
Ring of Nestor’s imagery. It examines individual motifs on the ring, such as 
human figures, animals, vegetation, and ritual objects, and considers their 
arrangement and interaction within the scene. The author situates the ring 
within wider discussions of Aegean glyptic forgeries and authenticity, noting 
that the ring has been subject to questions about whether it is an ancient 
artifact or a modern fabrication. The study compares the Ring of Nestor’s 
iconography and style with securely provenanced Mycenaean and Minoan rings to 
identify stylistic consistencies and anomalies relevant to its dating and 
authenticity. It outlines different scholarly interpretations of the scene, 
including readings as an epiphany, a funerary ritual, or a mythological 
narrative, and argues for a preferred interpretation based on iconographic 
parallels and contextual considerations.",
