rings, using the Ring of Nestor as one of several case studies. The paper cites 
Evans’s original publication on the Ring of Nestor and reviews how this ring has
been employed in arguments about Minoan religious iconography. Hudler considers 
how digital imaging and data‑driven approaches can reassess older 
reconstructions of complex scenes, including the Ring of Nestor, and addresses 
issues such as the reliability of traditional drawings and impressions. The 
article situates the Ring of Nestor within broader methodological debates 
concerning the interpretation of glyptic imagery, authenticity concerns, and the
evaluation of long‑standing iconographic arguments.",
      "source_ref": "s4"
    },
    {
      "local_ref": "d5",
      "document_summary": "Davis et al.’s paper “The Lord of the Gold Rings: The
Griffin Warrior of Pylos” reports on the discovery and analysis of a richly 
furnished grave near Pylos, known as the Griffin Warrior tomb, including several
elaborate gold rings. The study describes these rings’ iconography, 
craftsmanship, and archaeological context and uses them to reassess aspects of 
Mycenaean art and society. Within this discussion, the authors compare one of 
the Griffin Warrior rings (Ring 2) to the Ring of Nestor, noting close 
iconographic parallels, such as a woody ivy motif comparable to the vegetation 
growing from a tree on the Ring of Nestor. This comparison is explicitly used to
argue that such detailed correspondences support the authenticity of both rings,
suggesting that the Ring of Nestor’s imagery is consistent with genuine Late 
Bronze Age Aegean glyptic. By linking the ring from Pylos with the Ring of 
Nestor, the paper contributes to debates on the latter’s dating, cultural 
attribution, and authenticity.",
