  "sources": [
    {
      "local_ref": "s1",
      "url": "https://digi.ub.uni-heidelberg.de/diglit/evans1925",
      "title": "“The Ring of Nestor”: A Glimpse into the Minoan After-World and 
a Sepulchral Treasure of Gold Signet-Rings and Bead-Seals from Thisbê, Boeotia",
      "source_type": "journal offprint / monograph (Journal of Hellenic Studies 
supplement)"
    }
  ],
  "documents": [
    {
      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 offprint, digitized by 
Heidelberg University Library, presents a detailed study of a gold treasure from
Thisbê in Boeotia, with particular emphasis on the object he terms the \"Ring of
Nestor\". The work is divided into two main parts: (I) a description and 
analysis of the sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê (pp. 1–42) and (II) a broader interpretative section on Minoan afterlife 
beliefs and their reflection in glyptic imagery (pp. 43–75), followed by plates 
illustrating the objects. Evans describes the Ring of Nestor as a massive gold 
signet ring forming the centerpiece of the Boeotian treasure, found in a 
funerary or tomb-related context near Thisbê. He gives a detailed iconographic 
description of its multi-figured intaglio scene, which includes human figures, 
altar- or table-like structures, a tree, and a griffin or similar hybrid 
creature. Evans interprets this scene within the framework of Minoan religion 
and conceptions of the afterworld, arguing that it represents a Minoan funerary 
or afterlife scene that has been adapted within a mainland (Boeotian) setting. 
Throughout the study Evans assigns the ring to the Late Bronze Age and more 
specifically to the later phases of Minoan glyptic art, emphasizing strong 
stylistic and iconographic connections to Late Minoan signet rings from Knossos 
and other Cretan sites and to related Mycenaean material. He uses these 
comparative observations to support a Late Minoan/Late Helladic chronological 
