      "local_ref": "d1",
      "document_summary": "Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 monograph presents and 
analyzes a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals discovered at
Thisbê in Boeotia, with particular emphasis on one gold signet, which he names 
the “Ring of Nestor”. The work is divided into a descriptive section on the 
assemblage and a more interpretive section that frames the ring’s imagery as 
providing a “glimpse into the Minoan after-world”. Evans describes the find 
context in a tomb, catalogues the objects, and offers detailed formal and 
stylistic description of the ring’s multi-figure scene. He argues for a Late 
Bronze Age date and for strong Minoan, specifically “Minoan afterlife” and 
religious, influences on what he considers a Mycenaean funerary context. The 
monograph lays out a comprehensive iconographic interpretation of the ring, 
identifying altars, deities, and ritual scenes, and uses it as a key example in 
his broader reconstruction of Minoan religion and afterlife beliefs. It has 
since served as the foundational scholarly publication for the Ring of Nestor, 
frequently cited for its discovery circumstances, physical description, and 
interpretive framework.",
