glyptic. Within this volume, several contributions cite Arthur Evans’s 1925 
study \"The Ring of Nestor\" and use the Ring of Nestor as a case study in 
broader debates on Aegean iconography. These chapters treat the ring as a 
classic example of miniature sacred landscapes and ritual scenes on Minoan or 
Minoanizing gold rings, discussing its imagery, such as altars and possibly 
epiphanic or cultic settings, in relation to other rings. They reconsider 
Evans’s interpretations and dating within updated frameworks, addressing issues 
such as the reading of sacred sites in miniature, the relationship between 
Minoan and Mycenaean iconography, and the methodological challenges of 
interpreting complex multi-figure scenes. The volume as a whole highlights 
