#### 5a. “Gold Rings Found in Warrior’s Tomb Connect Two Ancient Greek Cultures”
– Jason Daley, Smithsonian Magazine (2016)

- **URL**: 
https://www.smithsonianmag.com/smart-news/gold-rings-found-warriors-tomb-connect
-two-ancient-greek-cultures-180960680  
- **Publisher / Author**: Smithsonian Magazine; Jason Daley.  
- **Date**: October 2016.  
- **Source type**: Popular science magazine article.

- **Content / relevance**:  
  Summarizes the Griffin Warrior discovery and describes the four gold rings 
with **Minoan iconography** found in a **Late Helladic Mycenaean tomb**. It 
explains the cultural exchange and suggests that Mycenaean elites adopted Minoan
religious imagery. It cites Stocker and Davis and mentions that these rings are 
discussed in their Hesperia article.

- **How it bears on the conflict**:
  - Supports the view that the Griffin Warrior rings are **Mycenaean artifacts 
with Minoan imagery**, a concept crucial to the Minoanizing framework in which 
Davis & Stocker place parallels to the Ring of Nestor.  
  - Reinforces the portrayal of Davis & Stocker’s interpretation as mainstream 
in recent scholarship.

#### 5b. “Lord of the rings” – UC Magazine feature (Rachel Richardson, 2016)

- **URL**: 
https://magazine.uc.edu/editors_picks/recent_features/lordoftherings.html  
- **Publisher / Author**: UC Magazine (University of Cincinnati); Rachel 
Richardson.  
- **Date**: Oct. 3, 2016.  
- **Source type**: University magazine / institutional communication.

- **Content / relevance**:  
  Presents the Griffin Warrior discovery for a general audience, focusing on the
spectacular four gold rings and their **Minoan iconography**. Notes that:
  - The tomb is undisturbed and dates to c. 1500 BCE (Late Helladic).  
  - The rings were crafted with extremely fine goldworking skills.  
  - The iconography is central to rethinking the relationship between Minoan and
Mycenaean culture, echoing the scholarly argument in Davis & Stocker’s article.

- **How it bears on the conflict**:
  - Provides accessible phrasing for the idea that the rings show Minoan imagery
in a Mycenaean context, aligning with Davis & Stocker’s **Minoanizing 
Mycenaean** framework which is used when comparing the Ring of Nestor.

