{"query": {"id": "20260628155832949255_821e24f9", "handle": "q3", "text": "“Ring
of Nestor” description material dimensions iconography museum catalogue", 
"importance": "core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628155936304517_d015fd7a", "type":
"Evidence", "text": "Vlachopoulos describes the Ring of Nestor as \"measuring 
3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, the fifth largest Aegean signet ring\" and identifies it as 
a gold signet ring.", "source_ids": ["20260628155934822266_2e5de9aa"], 
"citations": [1]}, {"id": "20260628155936372955_0ca45fda", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The \"Minoan Glyptic\" catalogue entry for Kakovatos (Messenia) lists: 
gold signet ring, \"Ring of Nestor\", length L. 3.27 cm; Ashmolean Museum, 
Oxford, inv. 1938–1130; date LM IB–II (c. 1530–1400 BC).", "source_ids": 
["20260628155934917200_3d0c0067"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628155936449246_54e8d936", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The American 
Journal of Archaeology review notes: \"While some may still harbor doubts as to 
the authenticity of the ‘Ring of Nestor’ (no. 277), other suspect pieces 
(including no. 336) are now ...\"", "source_ids": 
["20260628155934946696_9934f917"], "citations": [4]}, {"id": 
"20260628155936519350_db5c3aac", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Hudler’s paper 
refers to \"Gemmae dubitandae, such as the ‘Ring of Nestor’,\" as illustrating 
that once the objects’ archaeological properties are recognized, they become 
central to authenticity discussions.", "source_ids": 
["20260628155934978771_b355881f"], "citations": [5]}, {"id": 
"20260628155936587089_20e60f79", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The JSTOR article 
remarks in connection with certain seals: \"Jumper, the Ring of Nestor. One 
would like to know more about them—who made them, when and where they were 
bought, the principles of detecting fakes.\"", "source_ids": 
["20260628155935012000_d8fb8c0c"], "citations": [6]}, {"id": 
"20260628155936653881_1e01b454", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The Nestor 2020 
bibliography lists an entry: A. G. Vlachopoulos, \"The Ring of Nestor and the 
Quest for Authenticity,\" in F. Blakolmer (ed.), Current Approaches and New 
Perspectives in Aegean Iconography (Aegis 18, 2020), pp. 223–252.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628155935046367_847d9e7d"], "citations": [7]}, {"id": 
"20260628160133936540_99d6c5c1", "type": "Claim", "text": "The Ring of Nestor is
a gold signet ring from Kakovatos in Messenia, now in the Ashmolean Museum 
(Oxford) under inventory number 1938–1130, and assigned in the Minoan glyptic 
catalogue to the LM IB–II period (c. 1530–1400 BCE).", "source_ids": 
["20260628155934917200_3d0c0067"], "citations": [3]}, {"id": 
"20260628160134089274_6f9ce82c", "type": "Claim", "text": "Published scholarly 
sources give slightly differing measurements for the Ring of Nestor: the Minoan 
Glyptic catalogue records a length of 3.27 cm, while Vlachopoulos reports 
dimensions of approximately 3.29 cm in length and 2.18 cm in width and notes 
that it is the fifth-largest Aegean signet ring.", "source_ids": 
["20260628155934822266_2e5de9aa", "20260628155934917200_3d0c0067"], "citations":
[1, 3]}, {"id": "20260628160134214944_bca67b54", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 
Ring of Nestor has been the subject of detailed iconographic and stylistic 
analysis, with at least two scholarly studies (Vlachopoulos 2020 and the article
\"Some Observations on the Iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor'\") providing 
extended descriptions of its bezel scene and comparing its motifs with other 
Minoan and Mycenaean seals.", "source_ids": ["20260628155934822266_2e5de9aa", 
"20260628155934886510_6c8b75ae", "20260628155935046367_847d9e7d"], "citations": 
[1, 2, 7]}, {"id": "20260628160134343892_e49cc0d1", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"The authenticity of the Ring of Nestor remains debated in the scholarly 
literature: Vlachopoulos concludes that recent technical and stylistic analyses 
support its authenticity, while other scholars and reviews (including Hudler’s 
classification of it among \"gemmae dubitandae\" and an American Journal of 
Archaeology review) note that doubts persist.", "source_ids": 
["20260628155934822266_2e5de9aa", "20260628155934886510_6c8b75ae", 
"20260628155934946696_9934f917", "20260628155934978771_b355881f", 
"20260628155935012000_d8fb8c0c"], "citations": [1, 2, 4, 5, 6]}, {"id": 
"20260628160134535480_d5674d73", "type": "Claim", "text": "Scholarly discussions
of the Ring of Nestor emphasize that its archaeological properties—such as find 
context, documentation, and provenance details (who made it and when and where 
it was acquired)—are central to debates over its authenticity, yet these 
properties are not fully clarified in the available summarized material.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628155934978771_b355881f", 
"20260628155935012000_d8fb8c0c"], "citations": [5, 6]}], "offset": 0, "limit": 
50, "total": 11, "next_offset": null}
