{"query": {"id": "20260628160250353418_6b5369cf", "handle": "q1.2", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" gold signet ring dating authenticity Evans 1925 Late Minoan 
Late Helladic Davis \"Lord of the Gold Rings\" Griffin Warrior Ring 2 \"Some 
observations on the iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor'\" forgery technological 
study Mycenaean gold rings tholos tombs metallography 1925-2025", "importance": 
"core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628160357497285_32aae611", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The digitized Evans 1925 publication is described as consisting of two 
main parts: (I) “Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from 
Thisbê, Boeotia” (pp. 1–42) and (II) “A glimpse into the Minoan after-world” 
(pp. 43–75), followed by plates I–V, indicating a structured discussion of the 
Boeotian treasure and broader Minoan religious interpretations.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160357570194_6094d2ec", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans’s study 
identifies the Ring of Nestor as the central piece of a Boeotian gold treasure 
from Thisbê that is associated with a tomb or funerary context, situating the 
ring within a sepulchral assemblage of gold signet rings and bead seals.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160357659824_85625324", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In describing the 
Ring of Nestor, Evans emphasizes its form as a massive gold signet ring and 
notes a multi-figured intaglio scene combining human figures, altar or table 
motifs, a tree, and a griffin-like creature.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160357728951_84fb8103", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans explicitly 
frames the narrative around the ring in terms of Minoan religion and afterlife 
beliefs and argues that the ring’s scene represents a Minoan funerary or 
\"after-world\" image that has been adapted within a mainland Boeotian 
setting.", "source_ids": ["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, 
{"id": "20260628160357863341_7f7dd61a", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The summary
indicates that Evans repeatedly stresses a Late Bronze Age date for the Ring of 
Nestor and places it in the “later phases of Minoan glyptic art,” using 
stylistic comparisons to Late Minoan signet rings from Knossos and other Cretan 
sites and to Mycenaean material to support this dating.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160357939475_9163db25", "type": "Evidence", "text": "According to the 
content summary, Evans addresses possible doubts about the ring’s authenticity 
by emphasizing the consistency of its iconography and style with other Minoan 
and Mycenaean signet rings, thereby arguing that it is an authentic product of 
the Late Minoan/Late Helladic period rather than a modern forgery.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160551892173_59a32be1", "type": "Claim", "text": "In his 1925 study, 
Evans presents the so‑called Ring of Nestor as the central piece of a Boeotian 
gold treasure from Thisbê, associated with a sepulchral or funerary context that
includes other gold signet rings and bead seals.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160551985251_c1c97b03", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans (1925) 
describes the Ring of Nestor as a massive gold signet ring engraved with a 
multi‑figured intaglio scene combining human figures, altar- or table‑like 
structures, a tree, and a griffin or griffin‑like creature.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160552068644_a9f639aa", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans (1925) 
interprets the Ring of Nestor’s scene as depicting Minoan religious beliefs and 
a funerary or 'after‑world' image that has been adapted into a mainland Boeotian
context.", "source_ids": ["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, 
{"id": "20260628160552136351_e7ab9272", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans (1925) 
assigns the Ring of Nestor to the Late Bronze Age, specifically to the later 
phases of Minoan glyptic art, by emphasizing stylistic and iconographic 
similarities with Late Minoan signet rings from Knossos and other Cretan sites, 
as well as related Mycenaean material.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}, {"id": 
"20260628160552201489_ffbb93ff", "type": "Claim", "text": "Evans (1925) argues 
for the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor as a genuine Late Minoan/Late 
Helladic product by stressing the consistency of its iconography and style with 
other Minoan and Mycenaean signet rings, implicitly addressing potential forgery
concerns.", "source_ids": ["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]},
{"id": "20260628160552286534_b20e010e", "type": "Claim", "text": "The 1925 Evans
offprint on the Boeotian gold treasure is structured in two main parts—Part I, 
'Sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia' 
(pp. 1–42), and Part II, 'A glimpse into the Minoan after-world' (pp. 
43–75)—followed by plates I–V illustrating objects such as the Ring of Nestor.",
"source_ids": ["20260628160356958135_b804fece"], "citations": [19]}], "offset": 
0, "limit": 50, "total": 12, "next_offset": null}
