{"query": {"id": "20260628161211399485_696d46a3", "handle": "q1.4", "text": 
"\"Ring of Nestor\" gold signet ring authenticity Evans 1925 Minoan origin 
interpretation Davis \"Lord of the Gold Rings\" Griffin Warrior rings Late 
Helladic Mycenaean Minoanizing comparative study \"Some observations on the 
iconography of the 'Ring of Nestor'\" forgery hypothesis metallurgical analysis 
stylistic parallels independent assessments December 15 2025", "importance": 
"core"}, "items": [{"id": "20260628161342689268_adcf6125", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "Evans’s 1925 publication is structured in two parts, one treating a 
sepulchral treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals from Thisbê, Boeotia and
the other titled “A glimpse into the Minoan after‑world,” in which the Ring of 
Nestor is central to his reconstruction of Minoan beliefs about the 
after‑life.", "source_ids": ["20260628160906751306_081f4cde"], "citations": 
[24]}, {"id": "20260628161342762814_20298213", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In 
his illustrated plates and descriptions Evans presents the Ring of Nestor as a 
gold signet ring with a complex engraved scene, providing measurements and 
drawings that show multiple human figures, cult equipment, and landscape 
details.", "source_ids": ["20260628160906751306_081f4cde"], "citations": [24]}, 
{"id": "20260628161342835880_c820af0c", "type": "Evidence", "text": "Evans 
explicitly states that the Ring of Nestor reflects Minoan conceptions of the 
after‑world and argues that, on stylistic grounds, the ring belongs to the Late 
Bronze Age Minoan artistic tradition even though it was found in Boeotia.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628160906751306_081f4cde"], "citations": [24]}, {"id": 
"20260628161342902948_995da40b", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The HathiTrust 
catalog entry records the title as “ʻThe ring of Nestor;̓ a glimpse into the 
Minoan after‑world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and 
bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia,” with author Sir Arthur Evans and publication 
date 1925.", "source_ids": ["20260628160516218905_aecfba47"], "citations": 
[20]}, {"id": "20260628161342970434_75161e4b", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Davis and Stocker describe the Griffin Warrior tomb as a large stone‑built 
grave discovered in 2015 near Tholos IV at Pylos, dating to Late Helladic IIA on
the basis of pottery, sealings, and other artifacts.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161341139606_0ff54332"], "citations": [30]}, {"id": 
"20260628161343057090_6ff175da", "type": "Evidence", "text": "In their catalog 
of finds Davis and Stocker describe four gold signet rings from the Griffin 
Warrior grave, emphasizing that their engraved scenes display Minoan ritual 
imagery, including female figures in cultic settings and associated religious 
paraphernalia.", "source_ids": ["20260628161341139606_0ff54332"], "citations": 
[30]}, {"id": "20260628161343131745_0b47b3ba", "type": "Evidence", "text": 
"Davis and Stocker argue that the Griffin Warrior rings demonstrate a 
Minoanizing Mycenaean artistic koine, in which Mycenaean elites at Pylos 
appropriated and recontextualized Minoan ritual imagery within a Late Helladic 
funerary setting.", "source_ids": ["20260628161341139606_0ff54332"], 
"citations": [30]}, {"id": "20260628161343199403_32e16175", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "In a comparative passage Davis and Stocker list specific iconographic 
parallels between one Griffin Warrior ring and the Ring of Nestor, noting 
similar arrangements of female figures, cult tables or altars, vegetal and 
landscape motifs, and animal symbols.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161341139606_0ff54332"], "citations": [30]}, {"id": 
"20260628161343270398_47b39212", "type": "Evidence", "text": "On the basis of 
these detailed parallels, Davis and Stocker conclude that the Ring of Nestor 
should be regarded as an authentic Late Bronze Age ring participating in the 
same Minoanizing Mycenaean iconographic koine as the Griffin Warrior rings, 
arguing that a modern forger could not have anticipated the patterns observed in
the newly excavated grave.", "source_ids": ["20260628161341139606_0ff54332"], 
"citations": [30]}, {"id": "20260628161343341193_906caf59", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The ResearchGate snippet for the article \"The 'Ring of Nestor'\" 
states: \"Here new technical observations are presented which serve to support 
the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor. In 1995 the ring was once again placed 
on display…\"", "source_ids": ["20260628160906941706_d4ff5180"], "citations": 
[29]}, {"id": "20260628161343410645_64905dd3", "type": "Evidence", "text": "The 
Smithsonian Magazine article notes that the Griffin Warrior tomb at Pylos dates 
to about 1500 B.C. and that four gold rings found in the grave depict scenes in 
a distinctly Minoan style, illustrating a link between the Mycenaean grave 
context and Minoan imagery.", "source_ids": ["20260628161341192361_fb6076c8"], 
"citations": [31]}, {"id": "20260628161343479109_f7b6956c", "type": "Evidence", 
"text": "The UC Magazine article reports that the Griffin Warrior tomb near the 
Palace of Nestor was undisturbed and dates to roughly 1500 B.C., and describes 
the four gold rings from the burial as having scenes in a Minoan style that have
reshaped scholarly understanding of Mycenaean‑Minoan interactions.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628161341219086_0896ad6b"], "citations": [32]}, {"id": 
"20260628161409008219_b64c2292", "type": "Claim", "text": "Arthur Evans’s 1925 
study presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral treasure of gold 
signet rings and bead seals reportedly found at Thisbê in Boeotia, and describes
it in detail as a gold signet ring bearing a complex engraved scene with 
multiple human figures, cult equipment, and landscape motifs.", "source_ids": 
["20260628160516218905_aecfba47", "20260628160906751306_081f4cde"], "citations":
[20, 24]}, {"id": "20260628161409250493_53a3a29e", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Evans interprets the Ring of Nestor’s engraved scene as reflecting Minoan 
conceptions of the after‑world and funerary ritual, and on stylistic grounds 
assigns the ring to the Late Bronze Age Minoan artistic tradition despite its 
Boeotian provenance.", "source_ids": ["20260628160906751306_081f4cde"], 
"citations": [24]}, {"id": "20260628161409409893_482b1ede", "type": "Claim", 
"text": "Davis and Stocker describe the Griffin Warrior tomb near Tholos IV at 
the Palace of Nestor in Pylos as a large, undisturbed stone‑built grave 
discovered in 2015 and date it to the Late Helladic IIA period (around 1500 BCE)
based on pottery, sealings, and associated artifacts.", "source_ids": 
["20260628161341139606_0ff54332", "20260628161341192361_fb6076c8", 
"20260628161341219086_0896ad6b"], "citations": [30, 31, 32]}, {"id": 
"20260628161409573775_13775f7f", "type": "Claim", "text": "Four gold signet 
rings from the Griffin Warrior grave bear engraved scenes in a Minoan artistic 
style, depicting Minoan ritual imagery such as female figures in cultic 
settings, cult equipment, and associated landscape and vegetal motifs.", 
"source_ids": ["20260628161341139606_0ff54332", "20260628161341192361_fb6076c8",
"20260628161341219086_0896ad6b"], "citations": [30, 31, 32]}, {"id": 
"20260628161409792827_06abaa8f", "type": "Claim", "text": "Davis and Stocker 
argue that the iconography of the Griffin Warrior rings reveals a Minoanizing 
Mycenaean artistic koine, in which Mycenaean elites at Pylos appropriated and 
recontextualized Minoan ritual imagery within a Late Helladic funerary 
setting.", "source_ids": ["20260628161341139606_0ff54332", 
"20260628161341192361_fb6076c8", "20260628161341219086_0896ad6b"], "citations": 
[30, 31, 32]}, {"id": "20260628161409967335_e8acf65f", "type": "Claim", "text": 
"Davis and Stocker identify detailed iconographic parallels between one Griffin 
