   from the Griffin Warrior grave, emphasizing that their engraved scenes       
   display Minoan ritual imagery, including female figures in cultic settings   
   and associated religious paraphernalia. [14]                                 
 • Davis and Stocker argue that the Griffin Warrior rings demonstrate a         
   Minoanizing Mycenaean artistic koine, in which Mycenaean elites at Pylos     
   appropriated and recontextualized Minoan ritual imagery within a Late        
   Helladic funerary setting. [14]                                              
 • In a comparative passage Davis and Stocker list specific iconographic        
   parallels between one Griffin Warrior ring and the Ring of Nestor, noting    
   similar arrangements of female figures, cult tables or altars, vegetal and   
   landscape motifs, and animal symbols. [14]                                   
