   of Nestor was undisturbed and dates to roughly 1500 B.C., and describes the  
   four gold rings from the burial as having scenes in a Minoan style that have 
   reshaped scholarly understanding of Mycenaean‑Minoan interactions. [17]      
 • Arthur Evans’s 1925 study presents the Ring of Nestor as part of a sepulchral
   treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found at Thisbê in   
   Boeotia, and describes it in detail as a gold signet ring bearing a complex  
   engraved scene with multiple human figures, cult equipment, and landscape    
   motifs. [10][13]                                                             
 • Evans interprets the Ring of Nestor’s engraved scene as reflecting Minoan    
   conceptions of the after‑world and funerary ritual, and on stylistic grounds 
   assigns the ring to the Late Bronze Age Minoan artistic tradition despite its
   Boeotian provenance. [13]                                                    
 • Davis and Stocker describe the Griffin Warrior tomb near Tholos IV at the    
   Palace of Nestor in Pylos as a large, undisturbed stone‑built grave          
   discovered in 2015 and date it to the Late Helladic IIA period (around 1500  
   BCE) based on pottery, sealings, and associated artifacts. [14][16][17]      
 • Four gold signet rings from the Griffin Warrior grave bear engraved scenes in
   a Minoan artistic style, depicting Minoan ritual imagery such as female      
   figures in cultic settings, cult equipment, and associated landscape and     
   vegetal motifs. [14][16][17]                                                 
 • Davis and Stocker argue that the iconography of the Griffin Warrior rings    
   reveals a Minoanizing Mycenaean artistic koine, in which Mycenaean elites at 
   Pylos appropriated and recontextualized Minoan ritual imagery within a Late  
   Helladic funerary setting. [14][16][17]                                      
 • Davis and Stocker identify detailed iconographic parallels between one       
   Griffin Warrior ring and the Ring of Nestor, including comparable            
   arrangements of female figures, altars or cult tables, vegetal and landscape 
   motifs, and animal symbols, and use these parallels to place the Ring of     
   Nestor within the same Late Bronze Age Minoanizing Mycenaean milieu as the   
   Griffin Warrior rings. [14]                                                  
 • Based on the newly observed iconographic patterns shared with the securely   
   excavated Griffin Warrior rings, Davis and Stocker argue that the Ring of    
   Nestor is an authentic Late Bronze Age ring participating in the same        
   Minoanizing Mycenaean koine and contend that a modern forger is unlikely to  
   have anticipated these complex, later‑discovered iconographic structures.    
   [14]                                                                         
 • A technical study of the Ring of Nestor, as summarized in a ResearchGate     
   entry, reports new technical observations and states that these support the  
   ring’s authenticity; the snippet also notes that the ring was returned to    
   display in 1995, implying renewed institutional confidence in the object.    
   [15]                                                                         
 • Popular and institutional articles (Smithsonian Magazine and UC Magazine)    
   echo Davis and Stocker’s view that the Griffin Warrior rings’ Minoan‑style   
   imagery demonstrates close cultural connections and interaction between      
   Minoan Crete and Mycenaean mainland elites and note that these finds have    
   reshaped scholarly discussion of Minoan–Mycenaean relations. [16][17]        
 • Bibliographic records and Evans’s own publication confirm that the Ring of   
   Nestor was first treated in detail in a 1925 work by Sir Arthur Evans titled 
   "The ring of Nestor; a glimpse into the Minoan after‑world, and a sepulchral 
   treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," which is 
   structured into a section on the Boeotian treasure and a section centered on 
   the ring’s role in reconstructing Minoan beliefs about the afterlife.        
   [10][13]                                                                     

