 • Academia.edu 上的论文摘要说明，该研究“focuses on the iconography of the ‘Ring
   of                                                                           
   Nestor’”并将其纳入爱琴伪造与真实性争论的背景，指出通过人物、动物和场景构图的 
   分析来评估它是否符合特定历史阶段的爱琴艺术语言。 [3]                         
 • 摘要提到，作者回顾了过去关于“Ring of                                         
   Nestor”真实性的不同意见，一些学者认为它是正品，而另一些则指出其风格混杂和发现
   过程中的疑点，可能表明其为一个晚期伪造或受 Gilliéron 等人影响的作品。 [3]    
 • 该论文还指出，作者通过整理“Ring of                                           
   Nestor”的物理特征、尺寸和工艺特点，与确证为米诺斯或迈锡尼时期的金印章戒指进行
   比对，以提出对其年代和文化归属的新评估。 [3]                                 
 • Arthur J. Evans’s 1925 study titled "The ring of Nestor: a glimpse into the  
   Minoan after-world and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and        
   bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia," published in the Journal of Hellenic       
   Studies, is the first systematic academic publication on the Ring of Nestor  
   and the associated gold signet rings and bead seals from the reported tomb   
   near Thisbê in Boeotia. [1][19]                                              
 • Arthur J. Evans obtained the Ring of Nestor by purchase on the art and       
   antiquities market; according to his own account, the ring and a group of    
   gold signet rings and bead seals were reported to have been discovered in a  
   tomb context near Thisbê in Boeotia before entering the market. [1][18]      
 • In his publications on the Ring of Nestor, Arthur J. Evans describes the     
   object as a large oval gold signet ring with a complex engraved bezel showing
   multiple human figures, animals, and symbols, and he reproduces the imagery  
   in plates that compare the ring to Cretan Minoan seal art. [18]              
 • Evans interprets the Ring of Nestor’s imagery as important evidence for      
   Minoan religious beliefs and conceptions of the afterlife, assigning the ring
   to a Minoan or "Minoanized" artistic tradition and dating it stylistically to
   a Late Minoan phase corresponding to the Mycenaean period. [18]              
 • Later scholarship has re-examined the Ring of Nestor’s iconography, physical 
   characteristics, and reported discovery story, with some authors arguing that
   stylistic mixture and suspicious aspects of its market trajectory may        
   indicate a late forgery or a work influenced by early-20th-century restorers 
   such as Émile Gilliéron, while others still treat it as a genuine Minoan or  
   Mycenaean-period artifact. [3][20][21]                                       
 • Émile Gilliéron and his son produced drawings and replicas of the Ring of    
   Nestor (along with the Ring of Minos and other Bronze Age objects), and these
   reproductions circulated widely in early 20th-century publications and       
   exhibitions, influencing both scholarly and public perceptions of Greek      
   Bronze Age material culture and contributing to later concerns about         
   restoration practices, authenticity, and potential forgery. [20]             
 • The Ring of Nestor has been used as a comparative Mycenaean gold signet ring 
   in later studies, including analyses of the St. Louis Art Museum seal ring   
   and discussions of the Griffin Warrior tomb rings, but some modern authors   
   note that Evans’s "Ring of Nestor" example, as reproduced in The Palace of   
   Minos (vol. IV, fig. 915b), subsequently "fell into disuse" as a standard    
   reference in scholarly discourse. [21][22]                                   

Resolve conflict: Evans 在 1925 年的著作与期刊文章中将“Ring of                  
