   was accepted by Sir Arthur Evans as genuine, but many other scholars         
   condemned it as a forgery. [23]                                              
 • 该 JAEI 文章的标题使用了“Pseudo-Minoan Nestor                                
   Ring”这一表述，将戒指归类为“伪米诺斯”类别而非标准米诺斯制品。 [23]           
 • JAEI 文中从“Egyptian                                                         
   iconography”的角度逐一分析戒指的图像元素，并与埃及题材进行比较，以说明其图像 
   可能源自埃及传统而非纯粹的爱琴本地传统。 [23]                                
 • Evans 1925年的专著题名为："The ring of Nestor". A glimpse into the Minoan    
   after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals   
   from Thisbê, Boeotia。 [13]                                                  
 • Evans 将 Thisbê 附近出土的一批金质印章戒指与珠形印章称为“sepulchral treasure 
   of gold signet-rings and bead-seals from Thisbê, Boeotia”，并将 Nestor Ring  
   视为其中最重要的一件。 [13]                                                  
 • 在专著第二部分“II. A glimpse into the Minoan after-world”中，Evans 利用      
   Nestor Ring 的图像来重构米诺斯人的冥界观念，将戒指视为“a glimpse into the    
   Minoan after-world”的关键图像证据。 [13]                                     
 • Evans 将戒指年代定为晚期米诺斯或与迈锡尼早期相关的“Late Minoan /             
   Mycenaean”时期，并在文字中将其描述为“a genuine Minoan or Minoanising         
   product”。 [13]                                                              
 • HathiTrust 目录记录书名："ʻThe ring of Nestor;̓ A glimpse into the Minoan     
   after-world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet-rings and bead-seals   
   from Thisbê, Boeotia, by Sir Arthur Evans."，出版年份标注为1925。 [10]       
 • 《The Art and Archaeology of the Aegean Bronze Age: A                        
   History》目录中包含标题为“Fakes and                                          
   Dubitanda”的章节，归类在讨论爱琴青铜时代伪造品和存疑文物的部分。 [24]        
 • Cossa《Eschatology》的 OCR 文本包含片段：“... 'ring of Nestor' (of questioned
   authenticity) the scene is divided into ...”，在括号中直接将 Nestor Ring     
   描述为“of questioned authenticity”。 [25]                                    
 • Antiquity OCR 文本片段包含“Ring of Nestor, because it was found at Nestor's  
   city of Pylos ... fell into disuse and at last completely dis-               
   appeared.”之类句子，暗示作者解释某个“Ring of                                 
   Nestor”这一称呼的缘由，并说明该称呼或相关用法后来“fell into disuse”。 [26]   
 • ResearchGate 上《The ‘Ring of Nestor’》条目的摘要开头写道：“In the past the  
   authenticity of the so-called Ring of Nestor has been debated mostly in terms
   of iconography. Arguments against its ancient origin rest ...”。 [15]        
 • Arthur Evans treated the so‑called Ring of Nestor, a large gold signet ring  
   reportedly from a sepulchral treasure near Thisbê in Boeotia, as a genuine   
   Late Minoan or Minoanising Bronze Age object and used its imagery as a key   
   source for reconstructing Minoan religious and afterlife beliefs. [10][13]   
 • Modern scholarship has repeatedly questioned the ancient authenticity of the 
   Ring of Nestor, with many scholars treating it as a forgery or pseudo‑Minoan,
   and debates have focused largely on iconographic analysis rather than secure 
   archaeological provenance or convergent scientific tests. [3][23][24][25][15]
 • Modern iconographic and stylistic analyses suggest that the imagery on the   
   Ring of Nestor combines Aegean‑type motifs, such as incurved altars, with    
   heavy borrowing from Egyptian iconography and possible 1920s cultural or     
   aesthetic features, leading some scholars to view it as a pseudo‑Minoan or   
   hybrid work rather than a straightforward Bronze Age Cretan product.         
   [3][23][13]                                                                  
 • Because the Ring of Nestor lacks securely documented archaeological          
   provenance and has been subject to long‑running disputes over authenticity,  
   recent authors recommend treating it as a contested or dubitanda object: it  
   may retain heuristic value for studying Aegean forgery practices and hybrid  
   imagery, but should not be used uncritically as core evidence for Bronze Age 
   Minoan religion or eschatology. [3][23][24]                                  
 • Evans’s 1925 monograph ‘The Ring of Nestor; A glimpse into the Minoan        
   after‑world, and a sepulchral treasure of gold signet‑rings and bead‑seals   
   from Thisbê, Boeotia’ appears to be the first systematic publication devoted 
   specifically to the Ring of Nestor and its supposed discovery context,       
   iconography, and dating. [10][13]                                            
 • Independent scanned and OCR sources indicate that at least some early‑ to    
   mid‑20th‑century authors already referred to the Ring of Nestor as of        
   ‘questioned authenticity’ and noted that certain related terminologies or    
   usages connected with a ‘Ring of Nestor’ later ‘fell into disuse and at last 
   completely disappeared’, although the exact object and context of these      
   remarks remain unclear. [25][26][27]                                         

“Ring of Nestor” description material dimensions iconography museum catalogue   

 • Vlachopoulos describes the Ring of Nestor as "measuring 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm,  
   the fifth largest Aegean signet ring" and identifies it as a gold signet     
   ring. [28]                                                                   
 • The "Minoan Glyptic" catalogue entry for Kakovatos (Messenia) lists: gold    
   signet ring, "Ring of Nestor", length L. 3.27 cm; Ashmolean Museum, Oxford,  
   inv. 1938–1130; date LM IB–II (c. 1530–1400 BC). [29]                        
 • The American Journal of Archaeology review notes: "While some may still      
   harbor doubts as to the authenticity of the ‘Ring of Nestor’ (no. 277), other
   suspect pieces (including no. 336) are now ..." [30]                         
 • Hudler’s paper refers to "Gemmae dubitandae, such as the ‘Ring of Nestor’,"  
   as illustrating that once the objects’ archaeological properties are         
   recognized, they become central to authenticity discussions. [4]             
 • The JSTOR article remarks in connection with certain seals: "Jumper, the Ring
   of Nestor. One would like to know more about them—who made them, when and    
   where they were bought, the principles of detecting fakes." [31]             
 • The Nestor 2020 bibliography lists an entry: A. G. Vlachopoulos, "The Ring of
   Nestor and the Quest for Authenticity," in F. Blakolmer (ed.), Current       
   Approaches and New Perspectives in Aegean Iconography (Aegis 18, 2020), pp.  
   223–252. [32]                                                                
 • The Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring from Kakovatos in Messenia, now in  
   the Ashmolean Museum (Oxford) under inventory number 1938–1130, and assigned 
   in the Minoan glyptic catalogue to the LM IB–II period (c. 1530–1400 BCE).   
   [29]                                                                         
 • Published scholarly sources give slightly differing measurements for the Ring
   of Nestor: the Minoan Glyptic catalogue records a length of 3.27 cm, while   
   Vlachopoulos reports dimensions of approximately 3.29 cm in length and 2.18  
   cm in width and notes that it is the fifth-largest Aegean signet ring.       
   [28][29]                                                                     
 • The Ring of Nestor has been the subject of detailed iconographic and         
   stylistic analysis, with at least two scholarly studies (Vlachopoulos 2020   
   and the article "Some Observations on the Iconography of the 'Ring of        
   Nestor'") providing extended descriptions of its bezel scene and comparing   
   its motifs with other Minoan and Mycenaean seals. [28][3][32]                
 • The authenticity of the Ring of Nestor remains debated in the scholarly      
   literature: Vlachopoulos concludes that recent technical and stylistic       
   analyses support its authenticity, while other scholars and reviews          
   (including Hudler’s classification of it among "gemmae dubitandae" and an    
   American Journal of Archaeology review) note that doubts persist.            
   [28][3][30][4][31]                                                           
 • Scholarly discussions of the Ring of Nestor emphasize that its archaeological
   properties—such as find context, documentation, and provenance details (who  
   made it and when and where it was acquired)—are central to debates over its  
   authenticity, yet these properties are not fully clarified in the available  
   summarized material. [4][31]                                                 

